### The five rows 001 created, two at a time. They are still in the database — this case finds them by
### the same run-token prefix rather than by ids it would have to be handed.
###
### Three pages, so "they all fit on one page" cannot be why this passes.
# @name Page1
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/todos?title=like.{{runToken}}-page%25&order=title.asc&limit=2

### `after` carries the cursor the previous page issued — opaque, and minted by the provider
# @name Page2
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/todos?title=like.{{runToken}}-page%25&order=title.asc&limit=2&after={{Page1.response.body.$.next}}

###
# @name Page3
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/todos?title=like.{{runToken}}-page%25&order=title.asc&limit=2&after={{Page2.response.body.$.next}}

### The whole set in one request, so the three pages can be compared with the truth rather than with
### each other — three pages that consistently lost the same row would agree with themselves.
# @name WholeSet
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/todos?title=like.{{runToken}}-page%25&order=title.asc&limit=100

### The other paging mode: `offset` anchors the same window from the other end
# @name OffsetWindow
## TEST-EXPECT-STATUS: [200]
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/todos?title=like.{{runToken}}-page%25&order=title.asc&limit=2&offset=2
